[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_1
diagnosis_text:
The outage is caused by a northbound connectivity failure on leaf_router_0_1. Both uplink interfaces toward the spines are administratively down, so BGP cannot establish and no fabric routes are present on the leaf. Evidence: on leaf_router_0_1, eth0 (172.16.0.7/31 to spine_router_0_0:eth2 172.16.0.6) and eth1 (172.16.0.11/31 to spine_router_0_1:eth2 172.16.0.10) show state DOWN in ip addr; only eth2 (10.0.1.1/24) is UP. BGP on the leaf has both neighbors stuck in Active (show ip bgp summary), and the kernel routing table contains only the connected 10.0.1.0/24 network, confirming isolation from the fabric. Mechanistically, pc_0_1 can still ping its gateway (10.0.1.1) because that subnet is locally connected on eth2, but with both uplinks down, the leaf has no northbound path, so nothing else responds. Conversely, traffic from elsewhere (e.g., pc_0_0) cannot reach 10.0.1.0/24 because the leaf is not advertising it reliably over BGP and packets destined there are either withdrawn or blackholed. Localization: leaf_router_0_1 interfaces eth0 and eth1. Root cause: the uplink interfaces on leaf_router_0_1 were left administratively down. Corrective fix: bring up eth0 and eth1 (ip link set dev eth0 up; ip link set dev eth1 up), ensure they are set to auto-up in the startup network config, then let BGP re-establish to the spines so 10.0.1.0/24 is advertised and end-to-end reachability is restored.
=== END ===